<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for WSL Commentary.app under the hardened runtime.
  Owner: WP-6 (macOS port). Applied by build/ship-darwin.sh to the OUTER
  BUNDLE only — the vendored dylibs, the plugins and the three helper tools in
  Contents/MacOS are signed with the hardened runtime and NO entitlements.

  Notarisation requires the hardened runtime (codesign's runtime option), and
  the hardened runtime is a set of restrictions that are ON by default. Every
  key below is a hole in it, so each one has to earn its place from something
  this app actually does, and the ones that were considered and rejected are
  written down too — an absent key with no explanation gets added back by the
  next person who hits a wall.

  A TRAP, before anything else, because this file is a comment-heavy XML
  document and it caught this one on its first run: an XML comment may not
  contain a double hyphen, so no codesign flag can be written out longhand
  anywhere in here. codesign does not ignore it — it refuses the whole file
  with "Failed to parse entitlements: AMFIUnserializeXML: syntax error near
  line 9" and exits non-zero, which at least fails the build rather than
  embedding a blob AMFI would silently disregard at launch. Say "codesign's
  runtime option" and "the entitlements-dump flag", never the flags themselves.

  ── TAKEN ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  com.apple.security.device.audio-input

    This app's entire purpose is to capture a commentator's microphone and send
    it to a switcher. Two separate code paths touch the microphone: GStreamer's
    osxaudiosrc in the contribution pipeline, and getUserMedia inside the
    WKWebView. It is the one Resource Access key Apple documents for that.

    HONEST ABOUT WHAT WAS MEASURED, because the obvious experiment does not
    prove what it looks like it proves. Signing the bundle's own gst-launch
    with the hardened runtime and running

      osxaudiosrc num-buffers=20 ! fakesink

    reached EOS with the key AND without it. That is not evidence the key is
    unnecessary: a command-line process's TCC decision is attributed to the
    RESPONSIBLE process, which for anything started from a shell is the
    terminal, and the terminal already holds a microphone grant. A
    double-clicked .app is its own responsible process and gets no such loan.
    Proving the difference properly needs a TCC reset and a GUI capture cycle.

    So the key is taken on the evidence that is available: Apple documents it
    as the microphone entitlement for the hardened runtime, and all three
    notarised, non-sandboxed capture applications installed on the build box
    carry it (dumped with codesign's entitlements-dump flag on 2026-08-14 —
    OBS.app, zoom.us.app and Google Chrome.app; the flag is described rather
    than written out because an XML comment may not contain a double hyphen,
    and codesign fails the build with "AMFIUnserializeXML: syntax error" rather
    than embedding a blob AMFI would ignore).

    What actually produces the microphone PROMPT is the Info.plist string, not
    this key. Without NSMicrophoneUsageDescription the process is killed
    outright; see build/darwin/Info.plist note 1.

  com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation

    TAKEN, and this section is a CORRECTION. It sat under "considered and
    deliberately not taken" until 2026-08-16, with a careful measurement behind
    it, and the measurement was right about what it measured and blind to the
    only load that matters. What follows is the old reasoning, why it was
    incomplete, and what proved it.

    ── what was measured, and is still true ──

    Every Mach-O INSIDE the bundle loads without this key, and that was
    established with a control rather than by it happening to work. The whole
    bundle signed inside-out with the real Developer ID and the hardened
    runtime; then, with `env -i` and every GST_* pointed inside the .app, the
    bundle's own hardened-runtime gst-inspect asked for osxaudiosrc:

      Factory Details:
        Rank                     primary (256)
        Long-name                Audio Source (macOS)

    Control: one plugin, libgstlevel.dylib, re-signed ad-hoc so that it carried
    a different team from the process loading it, everything else untouched:

      module_open failed: dlopen(.../libgstlevel.dylib, 0x0006): code signature
      not valid for use in process: mapping process and mapped file
      (non-platform) have different Team IDs
      No such element or plugin 'level'

    So library validation IS enforced, and same-team signatures satisfy it.
    build/ship-darwin.sh signing EVERY Mach-O in the bundle is what makes the
    bundle's own payload load, and that stays true and stays required.

    ── what it was blind to ──

    THE BUNDLE IS NOT THE ONLY THING THIS PROCESS LOADS. libgstdecklink.dylib
    does not link Blackmagic's API; it holds one string,
    "/Library/Frameworks/DeckLinkAPI.framework", and loads it through CFBundle
    at first use. That framework is installed by Desktop Video, lives outside
    the bundle, and is signed by Blackmagic Design (Team ID 9ZGFBWLSYP), not by
    Sygnal TV Ltd (Team ID 5P76UVY5WF). No amount of signing our own payload
    can change that, because it is not our payload.

    The experiment above could not have caught it: it asked whether a bundled
    plugin loads, and every bundled plugin does. The load that fails is one the
    plugin performs afterwards, at device-enumeration time, from a path nothing
    in build/ ever touches.

    ── what proved it, 2026-08-16 ──

    A fitted, working UltraStudio 4K Mini, persistent-id 2747401380. Present in
    gst-device-monitor-1.0 as an Audio/Source device; present in an unsigned
    build of this application; ABSENT from the signed, notarised bundle, whose
    log read:

      gst: ListInputDevices: offered 4 of 4 Audio/Source devices (osxaudiosrc);
      0 were skipped

    Four where there are five, no skip, no warning, and NOTHING in the GStreamer
    debug log at any level, because from GStreamer's point of view nothing had
    failed: the provider probed and found no cards. The whole diagnosis was in
    the kernel log, which no operator will ever read:

      AppleMobileFileIntegrity: Library Validation failed: Rejecting
      '/Library/Frameworks/DeckLinkAPI.framework/Versions/A/DeckLinkAPI'
      (Team ID: 9ZGFBWLSYP, platform: no) for process 'wslcomms' (Team ID:
      5P76UVY5WF, platform: no), reason: mapping process and mapped file
      (non-platform) have different Team IDs

    The visible symptom at the desk was the Settings screen greying out the
    DeckLink video source and stating that no card was fitted, about a card
    whose tally light was on.

    ── why there is no narrower fix ──

    Apple offers no per-library waiver: this key is the only mechanism by which
    a hardened-runtime process may load a Mach-O signed by another team. The
    three alternatives were considered and all of them are worse:

      Re-sign DeckLinkAPI.framework with our team. It is Blackmagic's driver in
      /Library/Frameworks, shared with Desktop Video Setup and everything else
      on the machine that captures. Re-signing it on an operator's Mac is
      modifying another vendor's installed product, it breaks THEIR signature
      for every other application, and the next driver update silently undoes
      it. Not shippable.

      Ship without DeckLink support. The card is the commentary input and the
      camera path; that is the feature.

      Drive the card out of process. A helper that is not hardened, talking to
      the app over a socket, is a second binary to sign, notarise, launch,
      supervise and kill, to avoid one entitlement.

    ── what is given up, said plainly ──

    Any dylib signed by anyone may now be loaded into this process, for the
    life of the product. That is a real widening and it is bought for one
    named, necessary load.

    IT IS NOT A LICENCE TO STOP SIGNING THE PAYLOAD. build/ship-darwin.sh still
    signs every Mach-O in the bundle with our team, and must keep doing so.
    Before this key, that discipline enforced itself: an unsigned or
    foreign-signed file in the bundle failed to load and somebody noticed. Now
    it will load. The enforcement therefore moves entirely to ship-darwin.sh's
    own signing pass and its verification step, which is the thing to keep
    honest.

    A defence in depth remains on the application side: internal/gst's
    ListInputDevices now logs one line whenever the decklink plugin is loaded
    and enumerates no cards, and decklinkapi_darwin.go dlopens the framework on
    that path to say WHY in the log. If this key is ever lost, the failure is a
    sentence in the application log naming this file, rather than four devices
    where there are five.

  ── CONSIDERED AND DELIBERATELY NOT TAKEN ───────────────────────────────────

  com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit
  com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory

    The interesting one, and the one this file was WRONG about until the first
    end-to-end run of a real notarised bundle contradicted it.

    The easy half is still true: WKWebView runs its JavaScript out of process,
    in Apple's own com.apple.WebKit.WebContent, which carries its own
    entitlements and is not part of this bundle. That is why this app can hold
    a tighter set than a Chromium-embedding one, and it is not why these keys
    are absent.

    The half that was wrong was "nothing in GStreamer generates code at run
    time". liborc does — it is GStreamer's SIMD code generator and it is what
    videoconvert, videoscale, audioconvert and audioresample use for their
    inner loops. Running the real shipped pipeline out of the mounted disk
    image, with the hardened runtime and no JIT entitlement:

      ORC: ERROR: ../orc/orccodemem.c(345): orc_code_region_allocate_codemem():
      Failed to create write and exec mmap regions. This is probably because
      the Hardened Runtime is enabled without the
      com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit entitlement.

    The obvious reading of that message is "take the entitlement". MEASURED,
    2026-08-14, and the obvious reading is wrong twice over:

      1. THE ENTITLEMENT DOES NOT FIX IT. The same binary re-signed with
         allow-jit printed the identical error, and so did the same binary
         re-signed with allow-jit AND allow-unsigned-executable-memory. orc's
         message names allow-jit because that is the usual cause on x86_64; on
         Apple silicon a simultaneously writable and executable mapping cannot
         be obtained at all, entitlement or not — MAP_JIT with
         pthread_jit_write_protect_np is the only route and this build of orc
         does not take it. Taking the keys would have widened the runtime for
         a fault they do not repair.

      2. IT COSTS NOTHING. orc falls back to its C backup functions, and the
         pipeline runs to EOS either way. 300 buffers of
         png ! imagefreeze ! videoconvert ! videoscale ! I420 1280x720,
         four configurations, user CPU seconds:

           no entitlement                        1.64  1.62
           allow-jit                             1.65  1.62
           allow-jit + unsigned-exec-memory      1.62  1.65
           no entitlement, ORC_CODE=backup       1.63

         Indistinguishable. Whatever orc's JIT is worth on this workload, it is
         inside the noise of the encoder and the colour conversion around it.

    So neither key is taken. What IS done about it is a one-line environment
    change in internal/gst rather than an entitlement: ORC_CODE=backup before
    gst_init tells orc not to attempt the mapping in the first place, which
    silences an ERROR-level line that would otherwise appear in every log this
    product ever produces and send whoever reads it hunting for a fault that is
    not there. Same numbers, no message, no hole in the runtime.

    If a future GStreamer or orc adopts MAP_JIT properly, revisit this — with
    the benchmark above, not with the error message.

  com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables

    internal/gst sets GST_PLUGIN_SYSTEM_PATH_1_0, GST_PLUGIN_PATH_1_0,
    GST_PLUGIN_SCANNER_1_0, GST_REGISTRY_1_0 and GIO_MODULE_DIR before
    gst_init. None of those is a DYLD_ variable, and the hardened runtime only
    strips the DYLD_ family. The bundle deliberately resolves every library
    through @loader_path/@executable_path rewrites precisely so that no
    DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH or DYLD_FALLBACK_LIBRARY_PATH is needed — if anyone ever
    finds themselves wanting this key, the actual bug is a load command that
    build/bundle-gst-darwin.sh should have rewritten.

  com.apple.security.network.client
  com.apple.security.network.server

    This app is both — SRT out to the M2L-X, HTTP and HTTPS in on 80/443 for
    the remote-control page. Neither key is taken because neither does
    anything here: they are App Sandbox keys, and nothing in this bundle is
    sandboxed. Under the hardened runtime alone, sockets are not gated.

  com.apple.security.device.camera

    No camera. See build/darwin/Info.plist for the longer version; the short
    one is that the pipeline's video is a still PNG and both getUserMedia call
    sites ask for audio only.

  com.apple.security.app-sandbox

    Not sandboxed, and not casually addable. The app writes to
    ~/Library/Application Support/WSLComms and ~/Library/Caches/WSLComms,
    binds privileged-looking low ports for the remote listener, opens arbitrary
    CoreAudio devices and reads an operator-chosen slate PNG from anywhere on
    disk. Sandboxing it is a design change with its own work package, not an
    entitlement.

  com.apple.security.automation.apple-events

    Nothing here scripts another application.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key><true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key><true/>
</dict>
</plist>
